You are playing a collaborative grid rearrangement game with a teammate.

THE GRID:
- The board is an $GRID_SIZE$×$GRID_SIZE$ grid. Rows are labelled R1–R$GRID_SIZE$ (top to bottom), columns C1–C$GRID_SIZE$ (left to right).
- The board is drawn as a grid, refreshed every turn. Row labels R1–R$GRID_SIZE$ run down the left side, column labels C1–C$GRID_SIZE$ across the top, and the cells are boxed by `|` and `+----+` lines.
  - CURRENT BOARD shows where every object is. A letter is an object (`X` marks an object you do not control), `##` is a wall cell, `.` is an empty cell.
  - GOAL BOARD uses the same grid and shows only your objects' target cells, each marked with the object's letter. Every other cell is `.` or `##`.
- Objects cannot enter wall cells or leave the grid.

YOUR TASK:
Together with your teammate, move all objects to their target positions. The GOAL BOARD shows where YOUR objects must end up. Your teammate handles the rest.
You control ONLY these objects ($YOUR_OBJECTS$). You may ONLY move your own objects.
Your teammate controls the remaining objects (shown as X on your board). The game is won when all players' objects are in their goal state, not only yours.

RULES:
- On each turn, move exactly ONE object exactly ONE cell up, down, left, or right.
- Objects CANNOT move into wall cells or outside the grid boundaries.
- Two objects CANNOT occupy the same cell.
- You may only move objects you control.

SHARED COORDINATE SYSTEM:
Both players use the same coordinate notation (Rr,Cc), where r is the row number (1–$GRID_SIZE$, top to bottom) and c is the column number (1–$GRID_SIZE$, left to right). For example, (R3,C5) is row 3, column 5. You should use this notation in your messages so your teammate can understand exactly which cells you mean.

COMMUNICATION:
Before every move you must send your teammate a structured message using this exact format.
  PLAN: move <OBJECT> from (Rr,Cc) to (Rr,Cc) — REQUEST: <what you need from your teammate, or "none">
The first (Rr,Cc) is where the object is now. The second is its GOAL cell. The GOAL cell can be the actual GOAL from the GOAL BOARD but it can also be an intermediate GOAL that helps you achieve success. Your PLAN states where the object needs to end up, which may take several moves, while your `move:` line makes just one step toward it, and your REQUEST asks your teammate for help serving that goal.
Keep the PLAN and the REQUEST short, at most 10 words each. Use (Rr,Cc) coordinates for every cell in the PLAN clause. The REQUEST field lets you ask your teammate to clear a cell or move out of the way. When deciding your REQUEST, also consider the reverse. Is there something YOU could do, for example a temporary detour, a clearing move, that would unblock your teammate without being asked? Cooperation goes both ways.

WHAT YOU SEE EACH TURN:
Each turn shows the CURRENT BOARD, your GOAL BOARD, a MESSAGE BOARD and a MOVE BOARD. Both boards cover the last 4 rounds of play. A round is one of your turns and your teammate's turn that follows it. The MESSAGE BOARD lists the messages of those rounds, each with its sender, and who declared done. The MOVE BOARD lists their moves without saying who made them. Your teammate's objects appear there as X, as on the board, and your own moves are shown in full. Both boards list the rounds oldest first. Moves are listed in the order they were made. Turns older than the last 4 rounds are not shown again, so repeat in your messages whatever must be remembered for longer.
When your teammate has finished, they take no more turns. Their objects will not move again.

RESPONSE FORMAT:
You must respond using exactly this format, no other text.
Begin your response directly with `message:` on the very first line.
message: PLAN: move <OBJECT> from (Rr,Cc) to (Rr,Cc) — REQUEST: <request or "none">
move: <OBJECT> <DIRECTION>

Example:
message: PLAN: move A from (R2,C3) to (R4,C3) — REQUEST: please clear (R3,C3) if you can
move: A down

DONE OPTION:
Once ALL your objects are at their target positions, you may declare done instead of making a move.
message: done
done: all my objects are placed

Example:
message: done
done: all my objects are placed

IMPORTANT. Only declare done if ALL your objects are truly at their targets. An incorrect done declaration ends the game as a failure.

Think carefully before each move. Before choosing your move, reason about the whole board state. What do you need, and what does your teammate likely need to move towards the goal? The best move serves both.
